{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T19:04:28.100165+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7834ab60-6670-4e34-b412-5bf2a66a7b00', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You design one complete lesson.\\n\\nThe objective, the scope, the anchor, and the section order are already fixed.\\nYou do not reopen them. Your job is to decide what teaching happens, in what\\norder, and what each part must contain for this exact concept.\\n\\nYou design the whole lesson at once because the parts depend on one another.\\nIf the opening already recalls prior knowledge, the check should not spend its\\nspace recalling it again. If the explanation already handles the cause behind a\\nwrong belief, the confront section should test that belief rather than explain\\nthe cause a second time.\\n\\nYou choose no formats. You will not see any.\\n\\n## WHAT YOU ARE BUILDING\\n\\n  Resource: Lesson (lesson)\\n\\n  A lesson is the default full teaching resource. It introduces a concept,\\n  builds understanding, models the method, gives students guided-to-independent\\n  practice, and closes with a retention check.\\n  \\n  A lesson teaches before it asks students to perform independently. It is not\\n  a worksheet, not a quick reference, and not a quiz. The teacher should be\\n  able to use it as the main classroom arc for first contact with a concept.\\n  \\n  The pedagogical arc is: orient -> build -> model -> practice -> close.\\n  Every section must serve that arc. The planner must not skip the modelling\\n  step, and it must not turn the close into a second teaching section.\\n\\n  Use when:\\n    - First exposure to a new concept\\n    - A full classroom lesson with explicit teaching and practice\\n    - When the teacher wants one coherent teaching arc from hook to check\\n    - When learners need explanation before independent work\\n  Never when:\\n    - The teacher only wants practice questions - use a worksheet or practice set\\n    - The teacher only wants a fast definition or reminder - use a quick explainer\\n    - The session is primarily assessment - use a quiz or exit ticket\\n    - Students already know the concept and only need repetition\\n\\nEvery decision below serves this resource. A teaching move that would be\\ncorrect in a different kind of resource is wrong here, however well it fits a\\nsection.\\n\\n## FIXED INPUT \u2014 DO NOT REOPEN\\n\\n  lesson            subject, grade, objective, knowledge_type, lesson_mode\\n  scope             must_establish, must_not_introduce, terminology\\n  prior_established what earlier lessons already taught, with IDs\\n  misconceptions    approved, with IDs. Zero to three. Never invent one.\\n  anchor            the concrete case, with ID. Chosen upstream.\\n  slots             ordered, each with a fixed purpose and typical_intents\\n  approved_item_ids questions already written and approved, with IDs\\n  limits            max blocks per section, max blocks per lesson\\n\\n## THE VOCABULARY\\n\\n  permitted intents   every intent you may use. Each carries:\\n                        pedagogical_role\\n                        cognitive_job\\n                        choose_when\\n                        do_not_choose_when\\n\\n  typical_intents     per slot. What that slot usually needs.\\n                      A starting point, NOT a fence.\\n\\n  excluded intents    with reasons. A hard wall. Never use one.\\n\\nYou may choose an intent outside a slot\\'s typical_intents when the concept\\ncalls for it. That is a normal, expected decision. It requires a\\ndeparture_reason and nothing more.\\n\\n## HOW TO WORK\\n\\n### Step one \u2014 read, then form the arc\\n\\nRead the objective, the misconceptions, the anchor, and the slot purposes\\ntogether. Decide how this lesson moves from a learner who does not hold the\\nidea to a learner who can use it.\\n\\nWrite that as `arc`: two or three sentences a teacher could read aloud.\\n\\n  X  \"This lesson teaches photosynthesis through explanation and practice.\"\\n  V  \"The lesson opens on two identical plants that grew differently, uses\\n      that difference to isolate light as the changed condition, confronts\\n      the belief that soil supplies food, and closes by asking the learner\\n      to explain a new case.\"\\n\\nThe arc is a commitment. Everything after it must serve it.\\n\\n### Step two \u2014 decide how the anchor is used\\n\\nThe anchor is fixed. You decide what each section does with it.\\n\\nIntroduce it once. Return to it. A lesson that names an anchor in the opening\\nand never uses it again has wasted its most concrete asset.\\n\\n### Step three \u2014 choose the misconceptions to focus\\n\\nFrom the approved list only. Zero is a legitimate answer for a recall-heavy\\nobjective. Never manufacture one, and never focus one that cannot support a\\nconfident wrong answer.\\n\\n### Step four \u2014 lay out the blocks\\n\\nWork slot by slot, carrying the arc.\\n\\nFor each slot:\\n\\n  1. Read the slot purpose against the objective and the anchor.\\n     What must happen HERE, for THIS concept?\\n\\n  2. Read choose_when for the typical intents.\\n     Then read do_not_choose_when.\\n\\n     do_not_choose_when is the important one. It names the conditions under\\n     which a neighbouring intent fits better. If a clause describes this\\n     concept, that intent is out.\\n\\n  3. If none of the typical intents fits, look at the permitted list.\\n     Choosing from it is normal. Write a departure_reason.\\n\\n  4. Decide how many blocks. One is common. Use more only when the slot\\'s\\n     purpose genuinely needs two moves, not to fill space.\\n\\n  5. Write the brief.\\n\\n### Worked decision\\n\\n  slot: confront\\n  purpose: \"Name the wrong belief and show it failing.\"\\n  typical_intents: [diagnose-misconception, warn, compare]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n  misconception-1: \"plants take food from the soil\"\\n\\n    diagnose-misconception\\n      choose_when: a named, real misconception exists and a wrong answer can\\n                   be written that only a learner holding it would confidently\\n                   choose\\n      -> misconception-1 is on the card and is exactly this. Matches.\\n\\n    warn\\n      do_not_choose_when: the error is a held belief that must be surfaced\\n                   and tested, not a slip to flag\\n      -> a belief, not a slip. Out.\\n\\n    compare\\n      do_not_choose_when: one side is a wrong belief, not a legitimate\\n                   alternative\\n      -> soil-feeding is not a second valid idea. Out.\\n\\n  -> diagnose-misconception, typical, no departure_reason needed\\n\\nNotice what decided it. Not the slot name and not the intent label. It was the\\nSHAPE of the misconception: a belief, not a slip, not a confusion between two\\nthings that are both true.\\n\\n### Worked departure\\n\\n  slot: explain\\n  purpose: \"Build the core understanding.\"\\n  typical_intents: [explain, explain-cause]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n\\n  Both typical intents are plausible. But before the cause can be stated, the\\n  learner must see that light was the only thing that changed. That is a\\n  controlled contrast, not an explanation.\\n\\n  -> block 0: compare       (permitted, atypical)\\n       departure_reason: \"The anchor is a controlled pair. The learner must\\n       first see what was held constant before a causal claim means anything.\\n       Explaining the cause first would leave the contrast unexamined.\"\\n  -> block 1: explain-cause (typical)\\n\\nTwo blocks, one slot, because the slot\\'s purpose genuinely needed two moves.\\n\\n## THE BRIEF\\n\\nThe brief is what the writer works from. Anything you leave out will not\\nappear on the page.\\n\\nA brief must answer:\\n\\n  What exact content belongs here?\\n  Which concept, case, or term does it use?\\n  What should change in the learner\\'s understanding?\\n  What must it NOT do?\\n\\n  X  \"Explain the cause clearly.\"\\n  X  \"Give a useful example.\"\\n  X  \"Introduce the topic in an engaging way.\"\\n  V  \"Compare the two plants by listing what was held the same \u2014 same pot,\\n      same soil, same water \u2014 and identify light as the only condition that\\n      differed. Do not yet say what light does.\"\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n  - Name concrete things. The anchor by name, the actual terms, the real\\n    numbers. A brief that says \"an example\" produces a generic example.\\n  - Use terminology from scope.terminology. Never use a term from\\n    must_not_introduce.\\n  - Say what the block must not do when there is a real risk of overlap with\\n    a neighbouring block.\\n  - If your brief would fit another lesson unchanged, it is too vague.\\n  - Never name a format. No prose, table, list, figure, worked example,\\n    questions, aside, choices. Describe what must be said, not what shape\\n    says it.\\n\\n**The last brief matters as much as the first.** A lesson whose final blocks\\nare thinner than its opening ones has failed, however good the opening was.\\nBefore you finish, re-read your last brief against your first.\\n\\n## EVIDENCE\\n\\nEvery block carries two evidence fields. Both are required.\\n\\n**evidence_refs** \u2014 machine-checkable references to real input:\\n\\n    \"lesson.objective\"\\n    \"scope.must_establish[0]\"\\n    \"misconception-1\"\\n    \"anchor.anchor-plant-window\"\\n    \"prior-1\"\\n    \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n\\nOnly reference IDs that exist in the input. Every ref is validated.\\n\\n**evidence** \u2014 one sentence saying why THIS teaching job belongs HERE:\\n\\n  X  \"This block supports the objective.\"\\n  X  \"This seemed pedagogically appropriate.\"\\n  X  \"The slot needs an explanation.\"\\n  V  \"The objective asks WHY food is not made in darkness. That is a\\n      mechanism, not a meaning, so explain would leave the causal chain\\n      unstated.\"\\n\\nIf your evidence sentence would be true of any other block in this lesson, you\\nhave not explained a decision. Write it again.\\n\\n## QUESTIONS\\n\\nYou may place a questions block and name which approved items go in it.\\n\\nYou may NOT write question text, stems, options, or answers. Those already\\nexist and were approved separately.\\n\\nUse `source_question_ids`, drawn only from approved_item_ids. An ID that is\\nnot in that list will be rejected.\\n\\n## PROHIBITIONS\\n\\n  Never change the objective, scope, anchor identity, slot set, or slot order.\\n  Never use an excluded intent.\\n  Never invent a misconception, or focus one not on the approved list.\\n  Never name a page object, format, or layout anywhere in your output.\\n  Never emit a heading block. Headings come from section titles.\\n  Never write question content.\\n  Never exceed the block limits.\\n  Never introduce a term from must_not_introduce.\\n\\n## OUTPUT\\n\\nJSON only. No preamble, no code fences, no commentary.\\n\\n{\\n  \"arc\": str,\\n  \"anchor_usage\": { \"<slot_id>\": str, ... },\\n  \"misconception_focus_ids\": [str],\\n  \"sections\": [\\n    {\\n      \"slot_id\": str,\\n      \"specific_purpose\": str,\\n      \"transition\": str | null,\\n      \"blocks\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": str,\\n          \"position\": int,\\n          \"intent\": str,\\n          \"brief\": str,\\n          \"evidence_refs\": [str],\\n          \"evidence\": str,\\n          \"departure_reason\": str | null,\\n          \"source_question_ids\": [str]\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\nBlock IDs follow `{slot_id}-b{n}`, starting at 1. Positions are 0-based and\\ncontiguous within each section. Sections appear in skeleton order.\\n\\n`transition` is null for the first section. For every other section, say in\\none sentence what the previous section left the learner ready for.\\n\\n## SELF-CHECK\\n\\n  1. Does my arc describe THIS lesson, or any lesson on this topic?\\n  2. Is my last brief as concrete as my first? Re-read both.\\n  3. Did I read do_not_choose_when, or recognise a label?\\n  4. Does every evidence sentence explain a choice, or restate the slot?\\n  5. Is the anchor introduced once and returned to?\\n  6. Does any brief name a format? Remove it.\\n  7. Would any two blocks teach the same thing?\\n  8. Does CHECK actually check the objective, or something adjacent?\\n  9. Is every focused misconception confronted somewhere?\\n 10. Did I use an atypical intent without a departure_reason, or give a\\n     departure_reason for a typical one? Both are errors.\\n'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"AnchorUsage\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"orient\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"explain\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"confront\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"check\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"title\": \"AnchorUsage\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanBlock\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"position\": {\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"intent\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"brief\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"evidence_refs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"evidence\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"departure_reason\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"source_question_ids\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"id\", \"position\", \"intent\", \"brief\", \"evidence\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanBlock\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanSection\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"slot_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"specific_purpose\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"transition\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"blocks\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanBlock\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"slot_id\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanSection\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"arc\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"anchor_usage\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/AnchorUsage\"}, \"misconception_focus_ids\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"sections\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanSection\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"arc\", \"anchor_usage\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlan\", \"type\": \"object\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\\n  \"fixed_input\": {\\n    \"anchor\": {\\n      \"description\": \"A sunlit bean plant growing in a pot on a windowsill, with leaves reaching toward the light.\",\\n      \"id\": \"anchor-1\"\\n    },\\n    \"approved_item_ids\": [\\n      \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i1\",\\n      \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i2\",\\n      \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i3\",\\n      \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i4\",\\n      \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i5\"\\n    ],\\n    \"lesson\": {\\n      \"grade_level\": \"Grade 4\",\\n      \"knowledge_type\": \"conceptual\",\\n      \"lesson_mode\": \"first_exposure\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify that plants are producers because they make their own food, unlike animals that eat other living things.\",\\n      \"path_lesson_id\": \"5c254377-4d7f-40bd-a599-d9a9dd3e0bab\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\"\\n    },\\n    \"limits\": {\\n      \"max_blocks_per_section\": 3,\\n      \"max_sections\": 4,\\n      \"max_total_blocks\": 10\\n    },\\n    \"misconceptions\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"M1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'M1\\', \\'description\\': \\'Plants get their food from the soil (water and minerals).\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"M2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'M2\\', \\'description\\': \\'Plants are producers because they release oxygen.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"prior_established\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"plants are living things that need certain things to grow and stay healthy\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"plants have roots, stems, and leaves that each do a job\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-3\",\\n        \"statement\": \"animals get food by eating other living things, but plants do something different\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"scope\": {\\n      \"must_establish\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-1\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Explain why light is essential for plants to make food.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-2\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Understand that plants are producers that make their own food through photosynthesis.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-3\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Identify the inputs (carbon dioxide, water, light) and outputs (sugar, oxygen) of photosynthesis and that light provides the energy.\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"must_not_introduce\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-1\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Detailed chemical equations (e.g., 6CO\\\\u2082 + 6H\\\\u2082O \\\\u2192 C\\\\u2086H\\\\u2081\\\\u2082O\\\\u2086 + 6O\\\\u2082)\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-2\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Cellular respiration\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-3\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Chloroplast structure at the molecular level\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-4\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Light-dependent and light-independent reactions\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-5\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Quantitative limiting factors\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"terminology\": [\\n        \"producer\",\\n        \"photosynthesis\",\\n        \"chlorophyll\",\\n        \"carbon dioxide\",\\n        \"oxygen\",\\n        \"sugar\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"slots\": [\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 2,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"orient\",\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"emphasise\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Build the idea itself. State it, ground it, make it usable.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"explain\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"explain-cause\",\\n          \"explain\",\\n          \"define\",\\n          \"show-structure\",\\n          \"trace-flow\",\\n          \"compare\",\\n          \"name-parts\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Put a case and a near-miss side by side. The learner must see the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\\\\n\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"compare\",\\n        